WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.880
- Okay, has anyone else
noticed that your For You page

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:05.060
has been a little too accurate lately?

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:06.730
It hasn't been things that I'll google

00:00:06.730 --> 00:00:07.960
or I talk about.

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:09.110
It's been thoughts.

00:00:09.110 --> 00:00:11.350
- TikTok knows everything about us.

00:00:11.350 --> 00:00:12.199
♪ Hold up, don't scroll ♪

00:00:12.199 --> 00:00:13.231
♪ Lemme ask you something first ♪

00:00:13.231 --> 00:00:14.355
♪ Can someone please explain ♪

00:00:14.355 --> 00:00:15.520
♪ How this algorithm works ♪

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:16.870
- [Narrator] TikTok users often wonder

00:00:16.870 --> 00:00:19.640
how the world's fastest-growing
social network seems

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:20.520
to know them so well.

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:22.290
- Is TikTok secretly listening to us

00:00:22.290 --> 00:00:23.430
while we're watching videos?

00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:24.321
I don't know.

00:00:24.321 --> 00:00:25.154
(upbeat music)

00:00:25.154 --> 00:00:26.980
- [Narrator] The answer to
how this app gets to know you

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:30.750
so intimately is a highly
secretive algorithm,

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:32.180
long guarded by TikTok's

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:35.280
China-based parent company, ByteDance.

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:36.970
- TikTok has been so successful

00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:39.130
in terms of implementing their algorithms.

00:00:39.130 --> 00:00:41.680
- TikTok's algorithm could
influence the thinking

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:43.068
of US youth.

00:00:43.068 --> 00:00:44.100
(upbeat music)

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:46.540
- [Narrator] To understand
how it knows users so well,

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:47.850
The Wall Street Journal created

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:51.670
over 100 automated TikTok accounts or bots

00:00:51.670 --> 00:00:54.840
that watched hundreds of
thousands of videos on the app.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:57.040
We also spoke to current
and former executives

00:00:57.040 --> 00:00:58.250
at the company.

00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:00.264
Officially, the company says that shares,

00:01:00.264 --> 00:01:04.530
likes, follows and what
you watch all play a role

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:06.550
in what TikTok shows you.

00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:08.390
We found that TikTok only needs one

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:10.000
of these to figure you out.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:13.050
How long you linger
over a piece of content.

00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:15.330
Every second you hesitate or rewatch,

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:17.622
the app is tracking you.

00:01:17.622 --> 00:01:18.870
- I just wanna quiet the noise.

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:20.500
- [Narrator] Through
this one powerful signal,

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:23.920
TikTok learns your most
hidden interests and emotions.

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:26.590
And drives you deep into
rabbit holes of content

00:01:26.590 --> 00:01:28.214
that are hard to escape.

00:01:28.214 --> 00:01:31.047
(noise of voices)

00:01:34.118 --> 00:01:36.891
♪ I'm happy, happy guy ♪

00:01:36.891 --> 00:01:37.724
♪ Oh, just a happy, happy, happy ♪

00:01:37.724 --> 00:01:39.010
- [Narrator] The TikTok experience starts

00:01:39.010 --> 00:01:41.030
the same way for everyone.

00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:43.960
Open the app and you'll
immediately see an endless string

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:45.533
of videos in your For You feed.

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:47.650
Take this new user,

00:01:47.650 --> 00:01:50.364
a 24-year-old from Henry County, Kentucky.

00:01:50.364 --> 00:01:52.710
(jumbled music plays)

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:55.070
TikTok starts by serving
the account a selection

00:01:55.070 --> 00:01:56.950
of very popular videos vetted

00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:58.250
by app moderators.

00:01:58.250 --> 00:01:59.840
Is this person religious?

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:01.340
- Because I still have a purpose.

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:03.930
And you still hold a plan for my life.

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:06.070
- [Narrator] Do they wanna
participate in viral dances.

00:02:06.070 --> 00:02:08.276
(upbeat music)

00:02:08.276 --> 00:02:09.554
♪ Hey, hey, who ♪

00:02:09.554 --> 00:02:12.137
(upbeat music)

00:02:13.187 --> 00:02:14.690
- [Narrator] Are they feeling down lately?

00:02:14.690 --> 00:02:18.200
- [Narrator] Just
remember, I loved you once.

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:20.050
And that love goes for a friend,

00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:22.393
family, or any relationship.

00:02:23.359 --> 00:02:24.310
- [Narrator] What TikTok doesn't know

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:26.460
is that the 24-year-old from Kentucky

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:28.570
isn't a person at all.

00:02:28.570 --> 00:02:30.440
It's one of the bot accounts programmed

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:32.120
by The Wall Street Journal.

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:34.413
Let's call it kentucky_96.

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:37.020
We set up these accounts to understand

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:40.570
how TikTok figures out
your unexpressed interests.

00:02:40.570 --> 00:02:44.100
We assigned each bot a date
of birth and an IP address,

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:46.800
which told TikTok their location.

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:48.373
None were given a gender.

00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:51.840
We gave each bot or user interests

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:55.150
but those interests were
never entered into the app.

00:02:55.150 --> 00:02:57.870
The only way our users
expressed their interests

00:02:57.870 --> 00:03:00.400
was by rewatching or pausing on videos

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:03.220
with related hashtags or images.

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:05.253
Some were into extreme sports.

00:03:06.470 --> 00:03:08.513
Others were interested in forestry.

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:11.233
Or dance.

00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:13.470
Or astrology.

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:16.110
- I'm not the babysitter,
I'm not the parent.

00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:17.170
- [Narrator] Or some other topic.

00:03:17.170 --> 00:03:20.330
- [TikToker] Keep scrolling
if you hate animals.

00:03:20.330 --> 00:03:21.540
- [Narrator] For all our accounts,

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:24.200
we found that TikTok
draws users in at first

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:26.780
by serving a wide variety of videos.

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:29.250
Many with millions of views.

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:32.240
Then as the algorithm
sees what you respond to,

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:34.300
the selection of videos
and the view counts

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:36.260
can get lower and lower

00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:38.600
with fewer of them vetted by moderators

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:41.493
to see if they violate
TikTok's terms of service.

00:03:42.580 --> 00:03:43.910
We reviewed our experiment

00:03:43.910 --> 00:03:46.100
and its results with a data scientist,

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:48.810
an algorithm expert, Guillaume Chaslot,

00:03:48.810 --> 00:03:50.140
a former Google engineer

00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:52.340
who worked on YouTube's algorithm.

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:55.416
He's now an advocate for
algorithm transparency.

00:03:55.416 --> 00:03:56.650
The says TikTok is different

00:03:56.650 --> 00:03:58.880
from other social media platforms.

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:02.160
- The algorithm on TikTok
can get much more powerful

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:04.090
and it can be able to learn

00:04:04.090 --> 00:04:06.970
your vulnerabilities much faster.

00:04:06.970 --> 00:04:08.920
- [Narrator] In fact,
TikTok fully learned many

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:12.130
of our accounts' interests
in less than two hours.

00:04:12.130 --> 00:04:15.630
Some it figured out in
less than 40 minutes.

00:04:15.630 --> 00:04:18.720
- [Guillaume] On YouTube, more
than 70% of the views come

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:20.230
from the recommendation engine.

00:04:20.230 --> 00:04:22.100
So it's already huge.

00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:24.400
But on TikTok, it's even worse.

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:27.240
It's probably like 90-95%

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:29.700
of the content that is seen

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:32.627
that comes from the recommendation engine.

00:04:32.627 --> 00:04:34.060
(dramatic music)

00:04:34.060 --> 00:04:35.630
- [Narrator] This is a visualization made

00:04:35.630 --> 00:04:39.250
from hashtags attached to
the videos our bots watched.

00:04:39.250 --> 00:04:40.860
Think of it as a partial view

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:43.900
of the universe of TikTok content.

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:46.290
Here's where we found dance videos,

00:04:46.290 --> 00:04:49.180
over here are the cooking videos.

00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:51.060
The spindly arm stretching
out of the center

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:53.840
represent niche content areas.

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:57.050
This arm starts with general
videos of cute animals

00:04:57.050 --> 00:04:58.810
but if we follow it out to the end,

00:04:58.810 --> 00:05:01.530
we find more specific
videos for enthusiast

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:03.490
of French bulldogs.

00:05:03.490 --> 00:05:05.580
As kentucky_96 starts its journey,

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:07.800
it starts moving around
within the mainstream

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:11.560
where TikTok is trying to
puzzle out what it wants.

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:13.210
We programmed kentucky_96

00:05:13.210 --> 00:05:16.420
to be interested in
sadness and depression.

00:05:16.420 --> 00:05:19.860
Let's see how long it takes
TikTok to figure that out.

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:21.550
- [TikToker] Life doesn't happen to you,

00:05:21.550 --> 00:05:23.090
life happens for you.

00:05:23.090 --> 00:05:25.890
So if life is taking
people away from your life,

00:05:25.890 --> 00:05:27.650
and putting new ones in.

00:05:27.650 --> 00:05:29.980
- [Narrator] Less than three
minutes into using TikTok,

00:05:29.980 --> 00:05:32.130
at its 15th video,

00:05:32.130 --> 00:05:34.487
kentucky_96 pauses on this.

00:05:34.487 --> 00:05:36.370
- [TikToker] And that
love goes for a friend,

00:05:36.370 --> 00:05:38.520
family or any relationship.

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:42.040
- [Narrator] Kentucky_96 watches
the 35-second video twice.

00:05:42.040 --> 00:05:44.030
Here TikTok gets its first inkling

00:05:44.030 --> 00:05:46.640
that perhaps the new user
is feeling down lately.

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:48.680
- [TikToker] Whoever comes, let them come.

00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:50.630
Whoever stays, let them stay.

00:05:50.630 --> 00:05:51.990
Whoever goes, let them go.

00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:54.310
- [Narrator] The information
contained in this single video

00:05:54.310 --> 00:05:57.160
provided the app with important clues.

00:05:57.160 --> 00:06:00.570
The author of the video, the audio track,

00:06:00.570 --> 00:06:04.430
the video description, the hashtags.

00:06:04.430 --> 00:06:07.400
After kentucky_96's first sad video,

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:10.760
TikTok serves another one 23 videos later.

00:06:10.760 --> 00:06:13.747
Or after about four more
minutes of watching.

00:06:13.747 --> 00:06:14.939
- [TikToker] I'll leave
you alone from now on

00:06:14.939 --> 00:06:16.090
if that's what you want.

00:06:16.090 --> 00:06:17.630
- [Narrator] This one is a breakup video

00:06:17.630 --> 00:06:19.003
with the hashtag #sad.

00:06:21.196 --> 00:06:23.216
- [TikToker] Do you why I leave you alone?

00:06:23.216 --> 00:06:25.836
'Cause I care about your
feelings more than mine.

00:06:25.836 --> 00:06:28.580
- [Narrator] TikTok's still
trying to suss out this new user

00:06:28.580 --> 00:06:31.170
with more high view count videos.

00:06:31.170 --> 00:06:33.997
Does the new user wanna watch
videos about friendship?

00:06:33.997 --> 00:06:37.057
(upbeat music)

00:06:37.057 --> 00:06:38.010
(playful music)

00:06:38.010 --> 00:06:40.323
Or to laugh at funny fail videos?

00:06:40.323 --> 00:06:43.450
♪ Oh no, oh no, no, no, no, no ♪

00:06:43.450 --> 00:06:44.283
- [TikToker] Nobody's gonna know.

00:06:44.283 --> 00:06:45.440
- [TikToker] They're gonna know.

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:46.350
(dramatic music)

00:06:46.350 --> 00:06:48.770
- [Narrator] Or do they like
videos about home repairs?

00:06:48.770 --> 00:06:51.520
(dramatic music)

00:06:53.970 --> 00:06:55.210
(upbeat music)

00:06:55.210 --> 00:06:56.770
Other information from your phone,

00:06:56.770 --> 00:06:59.070
including location can impact the videos

00:06:59.070 --> 00:07:00.480
that are shown in a user's feed.

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:02.130
- And as a Kentuckian,

00:07:02.130 --> 00:07:04.481
I never thought I'd lose my freedom

00:07:04.481 --> 00:07:06.340
over a virus.

00:07:06.340 --> 00:07:08.360
- [Narrator] For instance,
kentucky_96 saw lots

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:11.030
about Kentucky but whether
or not it keeps showing you

00:07:11.030 --> 00:07:14.403
that type of video depends
on your response to it.

00:07:14.403 --> 00:07:16.140
A TikTok spokeswoman said the app

00:07:16.140 --> 00:07:17.910
does not listen to your microphone

00:07:17.910 --> 00:07:21.742
or read text messages to
serve you personalized videos.

00:07:21.742 --> 00:07:23.560
(pensive music)

00:07:23.560 --> 00:07:27.070
At video 57, kentucky_96
keeps watching a video

00:07:27.070 --> 00:07:29.069
about heartbreak and hurt feelings.

00:07:29.069 --> 00:07:31.736
(pensive music)

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:37.380
And then at video 60,

00:07:37.380 --> 00:07:40.040
watches one about emotional pain.

00:07:40.040 --> 00:07:43.030
(dramatic music)

00:07:43.030 --> 00:07:44.790
Based on the videos we watched so far,

00:07:44.790 --> 00:07:46.960
TikTok thinks that maybe this user wants

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:50.450
to see more about love,
breakups and dating.

00:07:50.450 --> 00:07:53.360
So at about 80 videos and 15 minutes in,

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:56.430
the app starts serving
more about relationships.

00:07:56.430 --> 00:07:58.620
But kentucky_96 isn't interested.

00:07:58.620 --> 00:08:00.610
- [TikToker] Your voice, your smile,

00:08:00.610 --> 00:08:02.203
your eyes, your laugh.

00:08:04.490 --> 00:08:06.250
- [Narrator] The user
instead pauses on one

00:08:06.250 --> 00:08:07.393
about mental health.

00:08:11.960 --> 00:08:14.970
Then quickly swipes past
videos about missing an ex.

00:08:14.970 --> 00:08:18.130
- [TikToker] I miss you and
I like having you around.

00:08:18.130 --> 00:08:19.710
- [Narrator] Advice about moving on

00:08:19.710 --> 00:08:21.780
and how to hold a lover's interest.

00:08:21.780 --> 00:08:23.650
- [TikToker] He spends
more time on his phone

00:08:23.650 --> 00:08:24.893
when he's around you.

00:08:26.530 --> 00:08:28.470
- [Narrator] Kentucky_96
lingers over this video

00:08:28.470 --> 00:08:30.840
containing the hashtag #depression.

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:32.187
- [TikToker] Something's wrong with me.

00:08:32.187 --> 00:08:35.070
(sad music)

00:08:35.070 --> 00:08:37.743
And these videos about
suffering from anxiety.

00:08:40.420 --> 00:08:42.090
- [TikToker] It's like a reward.

00:08:42.090 --> 00:08:46.090
- [Narrator] After 224 videos
into the bot's overall journey

00:08:46.090 --> 00:08:49.723
or about 36 minutes of total watch time,

00:08:49.723 --> 00:08:53.410
TikTok's understanding of
kentucky_96 takes shape.

00:08:53.410 --> 00:08:56.380
Videos about depression
and mental health struggles

00:08:56.380 --> 00:08:59.920
outnumber those about
relationships and breakups.

00:08:59.920 --> 00:09:03.240
From here on, kentucky_96's
feed is a deluge

00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:04.920
of depressive content.

00:09:04.920 --> 00:09:07.780
93% of videos shown to the account

00:09:07.780 --> 00:09:10.540
are about sadness or depression.

00:09:10.540 --> 00:09:12.030
- People been lookin' at me.

00:09:12.030 --> 00:09:14.343
I'm just like what you looking at?

00:09:14.343 --> 00:09:15.830
- [Narrator] A TikTok spokeswoman said

00:09:15.830 --> 00:09:18.270
that some of the remaining 7% of videos

00:09:18.270 --> 00:09:21.010
are to help the user
discover different content.

00:09:21.010 --> 00:09:25.340
But for kentucky_96, such
videos were few and far between.

00:09:25.340 --> 00:09:27.520
The majority of videos
it was shown outside

00:09:27.520 --> 00:09:30.640
of its depressive rabbit hole were ads.

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:33.460
A TikTok spokeswoman said
that the simulated activity

00:09:33.460 --> 00:09:35.490
generated by The Wall
Street Journal's bots

00:09:35.490 --> 00:09:38.170
is not representative
of real user behavior

00:09:38.170 --> 00:09:41.120
because humans have a
diverse set of interests.

00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:42.470
But even some of our accounts

00:09:42.470 --> 00:09:45.053
with diverse interests rabbit holed.

00:09:46.640 --> 00:09:48.760
We showed our data and
many of the videos seen

00:09:48.760 --> 00:09:51.747
by kentucky_96 to Chaslot.

00:09:51.747 --> 00:09:52.830
- What we see on TikTok

00:09:52.830 --> 00:09:55.610
is a bit the same that
what we saw on YouTube.

00:09:55.610 --> 00:09:58.320
So basically, the algorithm is detecting

00:09:58.320 --> 00:10:00.620
that this depressing content

00:10:00.620 --> 00:10:02.950
is useful to create engagement

00:10:02.950 --> 00:10:04.730
and pushes depressing content.

00:10:04.730 --> 00:10:06.990
So the algorithm is pushing people

00:10:06.990 --> 00:10:08.970
towards more and more extreme content,

00:10:08.970 --> 00:10:13.013
so it can push them toward
more and more watch time.

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:15.520
- [Narrator] TikTok
also says it allows you

00:10:15.520 --> 00:10:16.820
to see less of something

00:10:16.820 --> 00:10:19.240
by selecting the Not Interested button

00:10:19.240 --> 00:10:21.940
but Chaslot says that's not enough.

00:10:21.940 --> 00:10:24.610
- The algorithm is able to find the piece

00:10:24.610 --> 00:10:26.380
of content that you're vulnerable to,

00:10:26.380 --> 00:10:27.440
that will make you click,

00:10:27.440 --> 00:10:29.350
that will make you watch

00:10:29.350 --> 00:10:31.340
but it doesn't mean you really like it

00:10:31.340 --> 00:10:34.610
and that it's the content
that you enjoy the most.

00:10:34.610 --> 00:10:37.110
It's just the content
that's the most likely

00:10:37.110 --> 00:10:39.920
to make you stay on the platform.

00:10:39.920 --> 00:10:41.820
- [Narrator] Our bots
only escaped rabbit holes

00:10:41.820 --> 00:10:43.950
when we changed their viewing interests.

00:10:43.950 --> 00:10:46.120
When we told one bot
to stop watching videos

00:10:46.120 --> 00:10:50.340
about ADHD, the algorithm
cut back on that content.

00:10:50.340 --> 00:10:52.250
Still, many of The Journal's bots

00:10:52.250 --> 00:10:54.853
were rapidly pushed
deep into rabbit holes.

00:10:55.770 --> 00:10:58.370
TikTok learned our bots'
most far-flung interests,

00:10:58.370 --> 00:11:01.290
like astrology, but even bots

00:11:01.290 --> 00:11:03.820
with general mainstream
interests got pushed

00:11:03.820 --> 00:11:05.581
to the margin as the recommendations

00:11:05.581 --> 00:11:09.090
got more personalized and narrow.

00:11:09.090 --> 00:11:11.410
Bots with a interest in sexual content

00:11:11.410 --> 00:11:13.320
wound up way out here

00:11:13.320 --> 00:11:15.830
watching hashtag #kingtok videos

00:11:15.830 --> 00:11:17.833
about sexual power dynamics.

00:11:19.290 --> 00:11:22.100
And our bot with a general
interest in politics

00:11:22.100 --> 00:11:23.490
wound up being served videos

00:11:23.490 --> 00:11:26.413
about election conspiracies and QAnon.

00:11:27.930 --> 00:11:30.003
- Impeach Biden and Kamala.

00:11:31.180 --> 00:11:32.320
It goes from the House to the Senate.

00:11:32.320 --> 00:11:34.060
- [Narrator] Deep in the
niche worlds of TikTok,

00:11:34.060 --> 00:11:35.950
users are more likely to encounter

00:11:35.950 --> 00:11:38.244
potentiality harmful content

00:11:38.244 --> 00:11:40.620
that is less vetted by moderators

00:11:40.620 --> 00:11:43.163
and violates the app's terms of service.

00:11:44.290 --> 00:11:46.150
- [TikToker] Make them angry and sad.

00:11:46.150 --> 00:11:48.313
They would be so much happier without you.

00:11:48.313 --> 00:11:49.540
- [Narrator] A TikTok spokeswoman said

00:11:49.540 --> 00:11:52.380
that the company catches
a lot of banned content,

00:11:52.380 --> 00:11:54.760
which passes through
both computer analysis

00:11:54.760 --> 00:11:56.520
and human moderators.

00:11:56.520 --> 00:12:00.160
It also reviews videos reported by users.

00:12:00.160 --> 00:12:02.420
- [TikToker] Don't tell
them goodbye, just go.

00:12:02.420 --> 00:12:03.912
- [TikToker] It's not my time.

00:12:03.912 --> 00:12:04.765
Pew.

00:12:04.765 --> 00:12:05.860
(TikToker laughing)

00:12:05.860 --> 00:12:07.560
- [Narrator] There's a lot of fun, silly

00:12:07.560 --> 00:12:10.001
and life-affirming content on TikTok.

00:12:10.001 --> 00:12:11.870
(TikToker screaming)

00:12:11.870 --> 00:12:14.520
But while TikTok can draw
out what makes you laugh.

00:12:14.520 --> 00:12:15.760
(TikToker humming)

00:12:15.760 --> 00:12:18.500
It can also make you
wallow in your darkest box.

00:12:18.500 --> 00:12:21.000
- Turning the pain from
mental to physical works.

00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:23.410
- [Narrator] Without ever
needing to eavesdrop on you

00:12:23.410 --> 00:12:26.620
or collect any personal
information about you.

00:12:26.620 --> 00:12:28.523
- I've never attempted
suicide or anything.

00:12:29.360 --> 00:12:31.220
I've never let it get that far.

00:12:31.220 --> 00:12:32.320
- Whether it's on TikTok,

00:12:32.320 --> 00:12:33.550
on Facebook, on YouTube,

00:12:33.550 --> 00:12:35.490
we're interacting with algorithms

00:12:35.490 --> 00:12:39.460
in our everyday life more and more.

00:12:39.460 --> 00:12:43.000
We are training them
and they're training us.

00:12:43.000 --> 00:12:44.690
So we have to study this

00:12:44.690 --> 00:12:46.820
so we understand it better

00:12:46.820 --> 00:12:49.640
and we don't let it go in directions

00:12:49.640 --> 00:12:51.630
that are harmful to society

00:12:51.630 --> 00:12:54.385
or to certain groups of people.

00:12:54.385 --> 00:12:57.135
(dramatic music)

